r the tau-scaled scores can move at any temperature (zeroPriorOrderIsTemperatureInvariant). tau reaches the choice only through the UNSCALED habit prior (the commitment stated at policy.clj:225-229, 'controller temperature modulates G, never the habit prior'): on cautious (lnE 0, G 0) against habitual (lnE 1, G 2) the winner moves with tau (habitPriorMakesOrderTemperatureDependent; MEASURED production selects :cautious at tau=0.5 and :habitual at tau=8.0). This is the QUANTITATIVE reason for CommitmentTemperature.lean's live_selector_does_not_govern, which is imported and cited rather than reproved. MEASURED on the live strategic boundary: the action is the same at tau 0.1 and 9.0 while the recorded :tau and :softmax-weights both move. THE REVIEW WAS A REAL GATE AND FOUND FOUR THINGS, all fixed by this seat rather than re-dispatched (mathlib4 b31e5db2e4). THE SHARPEST: the delivered module dispatched over three laws and NEVER STATED THAT THEY ARE THREE -- there was no theorem anywhere that two arms differ on a shared input, so the finding the slice exists for was defined and not stated. This is the fourth consecutive slice whose review found exactly that. ALSO: the registry's own gamma = 1/beta was not mentioned in either module, so nothing connected the development to the :eq line it is about; adaptive-temperature was an opaque input field (spreadTemperature) rather than a computed function, which left the floor's stated purpose -- that identical EFE inputs cannot divide tau to zero -- unstatable, and the degenerate and empty cases unproved; and betaIsNotFloored had nothing to contrast with, the gain floor never having been shown to bite. A softer fifth: selectionLawControlsTemperatureChannel is true by decide of a Boolean the module itself defines, so it transcribes the dataflow rather than deriving it; it is left standing and zeroPriorOrderIsTemperatureInvariant is the derivation added beside it. GATES, bare exits: lake env lean 0 errors / 0 warnings on both modu